{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Design Draft and Theory-to-Prototype Alignment","title":"Wireframes That Show Learning Logic","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting wireframes that show learning logic to the annotated wireframe with per-region learner-action and sequence-phase labels, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-01","statement":"Annotate each wireframe region with the learner action it supports and its sequence position"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-02","statement":"Detect contradictions between a screen's purpose and its interaction affordances"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-03","statement":"Encode instructional intent through layout choices (button placement, feedback timing)"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-04","statement":"Critique a wireframe as a teaching decision, not as a visual arrangement"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment","ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"03","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/04/02"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Wireframes That Show Learning Logic\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Wireframes That Show Learning Logic, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/04-03-wireframes-that-show-learning-logic.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Wireframes That Show Learning Logic: Wireframes That Show Learning Logic, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA wireframe is usually treated as a layout document: boxes for where the text, image, and buttons go. In instructional design, that framing is a trap, because a layout can be perfectly arranged and still teach nothing or teach the wrong thing. A wireframe that shows learning logic annotates each region with the learner action it supports and its place in the instructional sequence, so a reviewer critiques the teaching decision rather than the aesthetics. AIL 606 asks for wireframes that expose intent: this region assesses recall, this control logs a help request, this feedback is delayed to keep performance unaided.\n\nThis section teaches you to annotate a wireframe so its instructional logic is visible before any visual design exists. You should leave able to tell when a layout decision quietly contradicts the screen's pedagogical purpose, as when a hint button undermines a screen meant to observe unaided performance.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Annotate each wireframe region with the learner action it supports and its sequence position.\n- Detect contradictions between a screen's purpose and its interaction affordances.\n- Encode instructional intent through layout choices (button placement, feedback timing).\n- Critique a wireframe as a teaching decision, not as a visual arrangement.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer presents a clean wireframe for a mastery-check screen: four answer options, a Hint button beside every item, and feedback after submission. It looks tidy. But the purpose of a mastery check is to observe what the learner can do unaided, and a hint button next to every item invites prompting that contaminates exactly the evidence the screen exists to collect. The layout is fine; the learning logic is self-contradictory. No amount of visual polish fixes a screen whose affordances fight its purpose.\n\nThe case shows that the most dangerous wireframe is the one that looks done. Tidiness hides the contradiction; only annotating each region with its instructional job surfaces it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA wireframe shows learning logic when every region carries two labels: the learner action it supports and where that action sits in the instructional sequence (acquire, practice with support, perform unaided, receive feedback). Layout is not neutral; placement and effort encode pedagogy. A help control placed prominently beside each item lowers the cost of seeking help, which is right for guided practice and wrong for a final check. The same control, moved to a single effortful button at the bottom that logs the request, raises the cost of help and preserves an unaided measure.\n\nThe governing idea is the alignment of affordance with intent. An affordance is what an interface invites the learner to do; intent is what the screen is pedagogically for. When the two align, the screen collects the evidence it promises. When they conflict, the screen produces misleading data: a \"mastery\" score inflated by hints, or a \"practice\" screen so bare that struggling learners get no support. Feedback timing is the other lever: immediate per-item feedback supports formative practice, while end-of-set feedback protects summative measurement by keeping each response independent.\n\n- **Acquire:** present and signal; affordances should reduce search and load.\n- **Practice with support:** make help cheap and visible; immediate feedback.\n- **Perform unaided:** make help costly and logged; delay feedback to the end.\n- **Reflect or feedback:** make the learner's own reasoning visible before revealing the answer.\n\nThe reason this matters for evidence is that a wireframe is the earliest point at which a teaching mistake is cheap to fix. Catching a hint-on-a-final-check contradiction in the wireframe costs a sentence; catching it after the prototype is built costs a rebuild.\n\nBefore you can label a region with its phase, you have to hold the instructional sequence in order. Arrange the four phases from first encounter to evidence of mastery.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Acquire: present and signal, with affordances that reduce search and load\", \"Practice with support: make help cheap and visible, with immediate feedback\", \"Perform unaided: make help costly and logged, with feedback delayed to the end\", \"Reflect or feedback: make the learner reasoning visible before revealing the answer\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A region labeled perform-unaided sits next to a prominent per-item Hint button. Why is this a learning-logic contradiction rather than a styling problem?\" explanation=\"The purpose of the screen is to observe what the learner can do without help, but a cheap, prominent hint invites prompting that contaminates exactly that evidence. The affordance fights the intent, which no amount of visual polish fixes.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A cheap prominent hint invites prompting that contaminates the unaided evidence the screen exists to collect.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The Hint button uses the wrong color for a mastery screen.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Hints should never appear anywhere in a module.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The region label is in the wrong font.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after revision of a practice-screen wireframe, the kind you would walk a reviewer through.\n\n**Before (wireframe of a mastery-check screen):**\n\n```\n+------------------------------------------+\n|  [Question text]                         |\n|  ( ) Option A   ( ) Option B             |\n|  ( ) Option C   ( ) Option D             |\n|  [ Hint ] beside each item               |\n|  [ Submit ]   feedback after all items   |\n+------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. No region is labeled with the learner action it supports, so this reads as a layout, not learning logic.\n2. A per-item Hint button on a mastery check invites prompting, which contradicts the screen's purpose of observing unaided performance.\n3. Feedback batched to the end fits a final check, but the per-item hints fight that intent.\n\n**After (revised wireframe with logic annotations):**\n\n```\n+------------------------------------------+\n|  [Question text]   <- assess recall of   |\n|                       Concept 4.2        |\n|  ( ) A  ( ) B  ( ) C  ( ) D              |\n|  [ Need help? ] one button, bottom,      |\n|     logs a hint request before scoring   |\n|  [ Submit ]  -> end-of-set feedback,     |\n|     so performance stays unaided         |\n+------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** each region now names the learner action and its place in the sequence (recall assessment, then optional help, then end-of-set feedback). Moving hints to one effortful bottom button encodes the assessment intent rather than decorating the screen. The reviewer can now critique the teaching decision, not just the look. The change is a clearer learning argument, not a prettier screen.\n\nYou are the reviewer for the tidy-but-contradictory mastery-check wireframe. Decide each affordance by the screen's stated purpose: measure unaided recall of Concept 4.2.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Reviewing affordances against the screen purpose\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The wireframe looks clean: four options, a Hint button beside every item, feedback after submission. The stated purpose is to measure unaided recall. What do you check first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Whether each affordance prominence and feedback timing serves the unaided-recall purpose\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Annotate intent first, then test the affordances against it.\"},{\"label\":\"Whether the spacing and alignment look balanced\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Aesthetics review declares a contradictory screen done. The logic was never checked.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The per-item Hint buttons invite prompting on a screen meant to be unaided. What is the fix that encodes the intent?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Replace per-item hints with one effortful bottom button that logs the request before scoring\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Raising the cost of help and logging it preserves an unaided measure.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep the hints but make them smaller\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"A smaller cheap hint still contaminates the measure; cost and logging are what matter.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"During pilot, mastery scores come back inflated because learners leaned on the hints. What does this prove?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Affordances that fight the purpose produce misleading data, so re-derive them from intent\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly; the screen collected the wrong evidence.\"}],\"feedback\":\"The contradiction surfaced as bad data.\"},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Now the feedback timing. The screen is summative. Per-item or end-of-set feedback?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"End-of-set feedback, so each response stays independent and unaided\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Delayed feedback protects summative measurement.\"},{\"label\":\"Immediate per-item feedback, since faster is friendlier\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Immediate feedback fits formative practice; on a final check it lets earlier items coach later ones.\"}]},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"Every region now carries a learner action, a phase tag, and an affordance whose prominence and timing serve the unaided-recall purpose. The wireframe is a teaching argument, not a layout.\",\"feedback\":\"Affordance aligned with intent collects the evidence the screen promises.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nAnnotate intent before you arrange boxes, then check that affordances serve it. Run three tests on every wireframe:\n\n1. **Action-label check:** Does each region state the learner action it supports?\n2. **Sequence check:** Is each region tagged with its phase (acquire, practice, perform, feedback)?\n3. **Affordance-intent check:** Does every control's prominence and feedback timing match the screen's purpose, or does any of them contradict it?\n\nIf a control invites behavior the screen's purpose forbids, the wireframe is misaligned regardless of how clean it looks.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe usual error is reviewing a wireframe for aesthetics (spacing, alignment, balance) and declaring it done, while the instructional logic was never stated and so was never checked. A second error is assuming that adding helpful affordances (hints, tips, examples) is always good; on an unaided check, help is contamination, not kindness.\n\nA wireframe is an argument about teaching, so review it for whether its affordances collect the evidence the screen promises.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBegin by writing the screen's purpose in one sentence before drawing anything: \"this screen measures unaided recall of Concept 4.2.\" That sentence is the standard every later decision answers to. Skipping it is how contradictions sneak in.\n\nNow label each region with a learner action and a phase tag. The act of labeling forces the contradiction to the surface; a Hint button next to a region labeled \"perform unaided\" is obviously wrong once both labels exist side by side. This is why annotation, not arrangement, is the real work of an instructional wireframe.\n\nFinally, tune the affordances to the purpose. Decide button prominence by the cost of the behavior you want: cheap and visible for help you encourage, costly and logged for help you want to discourage and measure. Set feedback timing by whether the screen is formative (immediate) or summative (delayed). When every affordance traces back to the one-sentence purpose, the wireframe shows learning logic.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is the learner action you think a region supports; the evidence is the annotation and affordance choice that make the action observable. By the end of AIL 606 04.03, your notes should let a reviewer judge whether each affordance serves the screen's stated purpose.\n\nA strong reader of an instructional wireframe can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is this screen's one-sentence pedagogical purpose?\n2. Which affordance, if any, contradicts that purpose?\n3. Why is the feedback timing right for this phase?\n4. How would the layout change if the screen moved from practice to a final check?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can read each region's learner action, its sequence phase, and a clear reason every affordance matches the screen's purpose. Treat visual styling as the last layer. Where an affordance and an intent conflict, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL to compare a practice-screen wireframe with a final-check wireframe rather than for generic layout tips.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I moved the per-item hints to one logged bottom button and delayed feedback to the end, because the screen measures unaided recall. I rejected the suggestion to add an example beside each item, since it would have contaminated the measure this screen exists to collect.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same wireframe from a summative mastery check to a first-attempt guided-practice screen on the same concept. Now the contradiction reverses: the costly, logged help button is wrong, because guided practice wants help to be cheap and immediate, and end-of-set feedback should become per-item. Transfer means re-deriving the affordances from the new phase, not reusing the layout. If you understand learning logic, changing the phase visibly changes where the help lives and when feedback fires.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the annotated wireframe with per-region learner-action and sequence-phase labels packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-03-wireframes-that-show-learning-logic-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"annotated wireframe with per-region learner-action and sequence-phase labels\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the annotated wireframe with per-region learner-action and sequence-phase labels.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the annotated wireframe with per-region learner-action and sequence-phase labels for AIL 606 04.03. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that every region carries a learner action, a phase tag, and an affordance whose prominence and feedback timing serve the screen's stated purpose. That alignment is what makes a wireframe a teaching argument.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic","stem":"What distinguishes a wireframe that shows learning logic from one that only shows screen layout?","options":["It uses high-fidelity colors and final fonts.","Each region is annotated with the learner action it supports and the moment in the learning sequence it serves, so the layout encodes a teaching decision.","It places the logo and navigation in the expected positions.","It includes every screen the module will ever have."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A learning-logic wireframe maps regions to learner actions and to where they sit in the instructional sequence. Polish and completeness do not show pedagogical reasoning.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_layout_without_logic","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-01","ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic","stem":"A reviewer says your wireframe is too detailed: it specifies exact pixel widths, final copy, and brand colors. Why is that a problem at the wireframe stage?","options":["High fidelity is never useful in instructional design.","Premature visual fidelity pulls review attention to surface choices and discourages changes to the underlying interaction and sequence, which is what the wireframe should still be testing.","Pixel widths are always wrong this early.","Brand colors must be approved by a separate team first."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Wireframes deliberately stay low-fidelity so reviewers critique flow and learner action, not polish. Over-fidelity locks in the layout before the learning logic is settled.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_premature_fidelity","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to generate a wireframe for a practice screen. It returns a polished layout with placeholder feedback shown only after the learner finishes all items. What is the strongest bounded use?","options":["Accept the layout because it looks professional and complete.","Use it as a starting block, then move feedback to appear per item if your sequence needs immediate correction, since the tool optimized for appearance, not for the learning interaction.","Reject all AI wireframes and draw everything by hand.","Ask the AI to also decide when feedback should appear and trust its choice."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI layouts optimize for plausible appearance, not for the interaction your sequence requires. The designer must place feedback where the learning logic, not the tool, calls for it.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_ai_layout_as_pedagogy","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframes_that_show_learning_logic_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Two wireframes show the same practice screen. Wireframe A places the hint button beside each item; Wireframe B hides all hints behind one button at the bottom. Which annotation would justify choosing B for a mastery-check screen?","options":["B looks cleaner with fewer buttons.","On a mastery-check screen the goal is to observe unaided performance, so hints are deliberately made effortful to reach; the layout encodes the assessment intent.","B saves screen space.","B matches the brand style guide better."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The right layout depends on the screen's instructional purpose. For a mastery check, making hints harder to reach is a defensible learning-logic decision, not a cosmetic one.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_layout_without_logic","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-03-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_layout_without_logic","pattern":"layout_without_learning_logic","description":"Presenting a wireframe as finished when its regions are not tied to learner actions or to a place in the instructional sequence","trigger":"wireframe shows placement and styling but no annotation of which learner action each region supports","feedback":"Annotate each region with the learner action it supports and where it sits in the sequence. Layout alone is not learning logic.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_premature_fidelity","pattern":"premature_visual_fidelity","description":"Adding final colors, copy, and pixel-exact sizing before the interaction and sequence are settled","trigger":"wireframe specifies brand colors, final copy, or exact pixel widths during early review","feedback":"Keep the wireframe low-fidelity so review stays on flow and learner action. Add visual fidelity after the learning logic is settled.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_3_wireframe_ai_layout_as_pedagogy","pattern":"ai_layout_treated_as_pedagogy","description":"Accepting an AI-generated layout as if its interaction choices encode sound instructional decisions","trigger":"learner keeps AI feedback timing or placement without checking it against the intended sequence","feedback":"Treat the AI layout as a draft block. Move feedback, hints, and actions to where your learning sequence requires them.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c7f8682a1f1bbe7497807f03c6097baec808128eceb77a84a6f543abc68f65eb","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}